-- 661_location_route_spot_repair.sql — location spots that stage a CAST dream on a route (a street, boardwalk, avenue,
-- trail, promenade, shrine approach) get a spot beside it, same place (2026-10-02, NIGHTLY_COMPOSITION_AUDIT_PLAN.md).
--
-- Why: a location dream's "set at" line is the whole spot text, and flux stages the person on what it names first.
-- Composition audit of delivered nightlies: solo location dreams on route-word spots were route-staged (person centred
-- on the route, the route receding behind) 29% vs 10% without. Render tests on Kevin's account before any row changed,
-- each spot replayed through the real nightly-dreams (force_slot_input, same model, same cast role) with the original
-- and the reworded spot: round 1, 10 spots x 2: route-staged 12/20 -> 4/20, swaps 20/20 both arms (the misses kept a
-- generic route noun in front or put the spot ON the route; the rewriter was tightened). Round 2, 12 new spots x 2:
-- 18/24 -> 0/24 by the vision tag, swaps 24/24 both arms, framing unchanged; by eye 2-3 of the 24 still read as a
-- corridor, from rewrites that kept a receding clause ("gravel path curving away"), which are rewritten by hand here.
--
-- 923 spots (scripts/fix-location-route-spots.js), every rewrite read by hand, ~85 edited by hand (no "corridor",
-- "tunnel", "receding", "winding" left in any). NOT applied: the race-track-garage card (59 spots), its rewrites
-- collapse into the same "pit wall / garage doorway" opening, so the card would lose its variety.
-- A spot that is also a scene-only postcard (pure_scene_eligible) keeps its text for postcards: the row is switched
-- off for cast dreams and a NEW cast-only row carries the rewrite (539 spots). A cast-only spot is rewritten in place
-- (384). The cast pool per card stays the same size; postcards are untouched.
-- Every statement is guarded (still the old text / the twin not there yet), so a re-run changes nothing.
-- ROLLBACK (the tool's --rollback writes it): split spot = DELETE the cast-only twin (location_key + new text,
-- pure_scene_eligible = false) and set the original row's character_eligible back to true; in-place = swap SET/WHERE.

